  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): An inverted yield curve, where short-term rates exceed long-term rates, historically signals market expectations of future economic weakness and potential recession. Investors accept lower long-term yields because they anticipate rate cuts to combat slowdown. Per COSO ERM market risk assessment, inverted curves have preceded most U.S. recessions.
Wrong (B): Strong economic growth is associated with a normal (upward-sloping) yield curve, not an inverted one.
