-- Q3075: Risk Management and Economics [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Futures lock in $80/barrel regardless of spot; options expire worthless at $72 spot, saving $250,000 overall","Futures are always preferred because they have no upfront cost and provide complete price protection","Both strategies produce identical results because futures and options are economically equivalent hedges","Options are always preferred because they cost less than futures in every possible price scenario"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 815, futures lock Redstone into $80/barrel ($4,000,000 total), overpaying by $400,000 versus the $72 spot price. With options, the calls expire worthless and Redstone buys at spot ($3,600,000) plus the $150,000 premium, totaling $3,750,000 and saving $250,000 versus futures. Futures provide symmetric price certainty; options provide asymmetric protection.
Wrong (B): Futures do have zero upfront cost but are not always preferred because they eliminate the ability to benefit from favorable price moves.
Wrong (C): Futures and options have fundamentally different risk-return profiles and produce different financial outcomes depending on price movement.
Wrong (D): Options involve a premium cost that makes them more expensive than futures when prices rise above the strike.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3075;

-- Q3077: Risk Management and Economics [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
