-- Q3168: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They are not eliminated because they represent actual cash transfers between legal entities","Only the income statement effect is eliminated; the cash flow statement shows the full amount","They are eliminated because the consolidated entity cannot pay dividends to itself","They are eliminated on the balance sheet only; other statements report them as external items"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 810-10-45, intercompany dividends are eliminated because the consolidated entity cannot pay dividends to itself. On the income statement, dividend income is reversed; on the balance sheet, intercompany receivables and payables are eliminated; on the cash flow statement, the transfer is excluded per ASC 230-10-50 since it is internal.
Wrong (A): Although actual cash moves between legal entities, consolidation treats the group as one entity, so internal transfers are not reported.
Wrong (B): The elimination applies to all three financial statements, not just the income statement.
Wrong (D): Elimination applies across all financial statements; selectively eliminating on only the balance sheet would create inconsistencies.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3168;

-- Q3169: Advanced Consolidations [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Eliminate the $60,000 gain and reduce depreciation by $12,000 per year","Eliminate the $60,000 gain only with no depreciation adjustment needed","Eliminate the $260,000 transfer price as intercompany revenue only","No consolidation adjustment is needed for intercompany equipment sales"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 810-10-45-1, the $60,000 intercompany gain ($260,000 minus $200,000) is eliminated, and the subsidiary''s excess depreciation of $12,000 per year ($60,000 over 5 years) is reversed to align with the consolidated cost basis. The depreciation correction effectively recognizes the deferred profit ratably over the asset''s remaining useful life.
Wrong (B): Eliminating only the gain without adjusting depreciation would overstate depreciation expense because the subsidiary depreciates from the inflated $260,000 transfer price.
Wrong (C): The $260,000 is the transfer price, not revenue; the gain is $60,000 and must be eliminated along with the depreciation impact.
Wrong (D): All intercompany profits on asset transfers must be eliminated until realized through transactions with outside parties.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3169;

-- Q3171: Advanced Consolidations [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both the intercompany receivable and payable are reported separately","They are eliminated because the consolidated entity cannot owe money to itself","Only the parent''s receivable is reported on the consolidated balance sheet","Only the subsidiary''s payable is reported on the consolidated balance sheet"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 810-10-45-1, intercompany loan balances must be fully eliminated because the consolidated entity cannot hold a receivable from or payable to itself. This elimination applies to both principal balances and any related intercompany interest income and expense.
Wrong (A): Reporting both balances would double-count within the consolidated entity and overstate both assets and liabilities on the balance sheet.
Wrong (C): Reporting only the receivable would overstate consolidated assets and omit the offsetting payable that nets to zero within the entity.
Wrong (D): Reporting only the payable would overstate consolidated liabilities and omit the offsetting receivable that nets to zero within the entity.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3171;

-- Q3172: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Business combinations requiring purchase price allocation","Other comprehensive income items bypassing net income","Revenue transactions recognized in the income statement","Equity transactions with no gain or loss recognized in earnings"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 810-10-45-23, changes in a parent''s ownership interest that do not result in loss of control are accounted for as equity transactions. No gain or loss is recognized in earnings because the parent retains its controlling financial interest, with any difference recorded in additional paid-in capital.
Wrong (A): Business combination accounting under ASC 805 applies only when control is obtained, not when ownership changes within an existing controlled subsidiary.
Wrong (B): These transactions are recorded directly in equity (APIC), not through other comprehensive income.
Wrong (C): Ownership changes within a controlled subsidiary are capital transactions between owners, not revenue-generating activities.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3172;

-- Q3175: Advanced Consolidations [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Dividends received from the investee recognized as revenue income","Only the changes in fair value of the investment on the balance sheet","The investee''s revenue on a proportional basis as the investor''s income","Its share of investee net income as income, and dividends as a return of investment"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 323-10-35, the equity method requires the investor to recognize its proportionate share of the investee''s net income as investment income, increasing the investment account. Dividends received reduce the investment balance rather than being recorded as revenue, reflecting a return of the investor''s capital.
Wrong (A): Recognizing dividends as revenue is the cost method approach, not the equity method; equity method treats dividends as reducing the investment balance.
Wrong (B): Fair value changes apply to investments measured at fair value under ASC 321, not equity method investments under ASC 323.
Wrong (C): The equity method uses the investee''s net income, not its revenue, to determine the investor''s share of earnings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3175;

-- Q3177: Advanced Consolidations [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Credits the investment account and debits subsidiary equity to eliminate both","Debits the investment account for the full $5,000,000 balance amount","Records additional goodwill of $5,000,000 on the consolidated balance sheet","Records $5,000,000 as revenue in the consolidated income statement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): The basic consolidation entry eliminates the parent''s investment account (credit) against the subsidiary''s equity accounts (debit). Since the parent owns 100% and the investment equals the subsidiary''s equity at $5,000,000, the entry balances exactly. Any difference would be allocated to fair value adjustments or goodwill.
Wrong (B): Debiting the investment account would increase it rather than eliminate it; the investment must be credited to remove it from the consolidated balance sheet.
Wrong (C): Goodwill arises only when acquisition cost exceeds the fair value of net identifiable assets, not from the elimination entry when investment equals equity.
Wrong (D): The consolidation entry is a worksheet elimination, not a revenue transaction; it removes reciprocal balances between parent and subsidiary.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3177;
